{%- comment -%}
  OnRepeat — display-currency mode. Emits exactly `true` or `false`.

  This is the single fact that decides TWO things which must never disagree:

    1. whether the shopper is shown USD prices, and
    2. which checkout they get.

  The rule, set by the merchant: Shopify's native checkout runs only while USD
  pricing is on screen. Any shopper seeing INR — which is every shopper in
  India, and every US shopper who has not switched market — goes to Razorpay
  Magic Checkout, so COD, UPI and the Indian address flow stay intact.

  Coupling them to one value is the safety property. If the checkout gate were
  written out a second time by hand it could drift from the price condition,
  and a drift in either direction is a live bug:

    prices INR + native checkout  → an Indian shopper silently loses COD/UPI
    prices USD + Magic Checkout   → a US shopper meets an OTP wall on a +91
                                    field and an India-shaped address form

  The condition mirrors snippets/money-display.liquid:32, which is the price
  authority for everything a shopper sees before checkout. That snippet is not
  refactored to call this one: it runs hundreds of times per page and a render
  per call would cost more than the duplication saves. If you ever change the
  condition there, change it here in the same commit — checkout routing rides
  on it.

  Usage (the theme's own idiom, see the first line of layout/theme.liquid):
    {%- capture usd_mode_raw -%}{%- render 'onrepeat-usd-mode' -%}{%- endcapture -%}
    {%- assign usd_mode = usd_mode_raw | strip -%}
    {%- if usd_mode == 'true' -%}…{%- endif -%}

  `strip` is not optional — a stray newline in this file would otherwise make
  every comparison false, which would silently route USD shoppers to Magic.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- liquid
  assign um_rate = settings.usd_inr_rate | default: 0 | round
  if settings.usd_display_enabled and um_rate > 0 and localization.country.iso_code == 'US'
    echo 'true'
  else
    echo 'false'
  endif
-%}
